# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# api/.env.example — template WITHOUT values.
#
# The real `.env` is in .gitignore and is never committed. `@nestjs/config`
# loads it with an ABSOLUTE path derived from `__dirname`, not from the
# working directory: the hosting starts the process in the user's HOME.
#
# The API validates these at boot against ONE schema, `src/common/config/env.ts`,
# and refuses to start with a list of what is missing or invalid. This file
# lists exactly the variables of that schema, no more and no fewer; a test
# (`env.spec.ts`) fails otherwise. What only the scripts read lives in
# `.env.migrate.example`.
#
# ── Old names, in transition (step 7.2-r3) ───────────────────────────────────
# Five variables moved to English: SOPORTES_DIR → RECEIPTS_DIR,
# SOPORTES_STORAGE → RECEIPTS_STORAGE, SOPORTES_BUCKET → RECEIPTS_BUCKET,
# PERMITIR_BASE_REMOTA → ALLOW_REMOTE_DATABASE and PERMITIR_AUTH_DESTRUCTIVA →
# ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE_AUTH. The values do not change (`si` is still `si`).
# Until the contraction (7.10) the API reads the old name if the new one is
# missing and warns about it in the log; if both are there with different
# values, the new one wins. A local `.env` with the old names still boots:
# rename them when you can.
#
# ── In LOCAL this points to the LOCAL database ───────────────────────────────
# Never to Supabase. The production database is used only from explicit
# files —`.env.supabase` for migrations, `.env.migrate` for the local one—
# and never by default. The API refuses to start if it detects otherwise
# (see `whyRefuseToStart` in `src/common/env.ts`).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Environment ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `development`, `test` or `production`. Outside production a local database
# is required.
NODE_ENV=
# `si` lifts that requirement. It is the escape valve for when pointing to a
# remote database is deliberate; any other value does not count.
ALLOW_REMOTE_DATABASE=
# Port the API listens on.
PORT=
# `debug`, `log`, `warn` or `error`.
LOG_LEVEL=
# Folder of the JSON log file (`api.log`, rotated). Without it: in production
# `~/logs/coco-api` (outside the release folder, which each deploy
# replaces); outside production, the console only.
LOG_DIR=

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Postgres the application connects through, as the `coco_app` role (without
# BYPASSRLS; scripts/db/create-app-role.sh creates it). On Supabase, through
# the pooler the user is `coco_app.<project-ref>`. See docs/runbook.md,
# "Row-level security".
DATABASE_URL=
# DIRECT connection, without the pooler, as the OWNER of the tables (with
# BYPASSRLS). Prisma migrations use it.
DIRECT_URL=

# ── Supabase Auth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Project URL. The GoTrue endpoint and the JWKS that verifies the tokens come
# from here.
SUPABASE_URL=
# Public key. Used for the operations on behalf of whoever has signed in.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Service key. SECRET: it creates users, changes passwords and revokes
# sessions. It never leaves the backend nor gets into a frontend bundle.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# `si` allows creating, deleting and changing the password of REAL accounts
# from a session that is not production. Off, those four operations are
# refused; signing in still works. It is turned on the day there is a separate
# Supabase project for development.
ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE_AUTH=

# ── Session and access ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Whoever signs up with this email is born an administrator and active. RISK:
# sign-up does not verify the email, so whoever types it first is admin. Set
# it only to create the first admin and empty it as soon as it exists.
# Here it is empty, always.
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL=
# `true` checks passwords against the breached-passwords database. Set it to
# `false` only in environments without internet access.
CHECK_BREACHED_PASSWORDS=
# Exact frontend origins, comma-separated. No wildcards.
CORS_ORIGINS=
# How many proxies sit in front of the API (LiteSpeed is one). Empty = 1. The
# client IP seen by the rate limiter and `audit_log` comes from it; with a CDN
# in front it would be 2. Never more than there are: the IP would become
# whatever the client writes in `X-Forwarded-For`.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=
# `true` logs the SHAPE of the proxy headers (how many entries, which one
# matches `X-Real-IP`; never an IP) on the first 20 requests of each
# process. Temporary diagnostic to confirm TRUST_PROXY_HOPS; empty afterwards.
LOG_PROXY_HEADERS=

# ── Receipts (the attached receipts) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Store folder, OUTSIDE the web tree. Locally, a folder of the repository so
# that the database and the files live in the same environment; without it,
# it falls back to `api/.soportes`.
RECEIPTS_DIR=
# Where the files live: `supabase` (private Supabase Storage bucket, with
# SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY) or `disk` (RECEIPTS_DIR). Without
# it: `supabase` in production and `disk` everywhere else.
RECEIPTS_STORAGE=
# Bucket name. `soportes` by default: the variable name changed, not the
# bucket's (ADR 0026).
RECEIPTS_BUCKET=
# In tests: `on` turns on the daily automatic-charges job, which is off with
# NODE_ENV=test (the e2e tests call it by hand).
AUTO_CHARGE=

# ── Static files ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Folder of the already-built frontend, to serve it from the API itself. Only
# needed when both go together in the same process.
SPA_DIST_PATH=

# ── Feature flags ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Flags turned on for ALL users, comma-separated (`flag_a,flag_b`). Each name
# has to be in packages/flags/src/registry.ts: with an unknown one the API
# does not boot. For a single user it does not go here: it goes in
# user_preferences with the key `feature:<name>` (see CONTRIBUTING.md,
# "Feature flags").
FEATURES=
